  <sys:String x:Key="Settings.PttExplanation">The key marks out the command. No false trigger is possible, at the cost of a busy finger. The microphone stays open the whole time — opening it on the press would cost 419 ms and clip the start of every sentence.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Settings.AlwaysOnExplanation">The grammar only accepts sentences that start with the wake word: ordinary conversation matches no alternative and is rejected without even being transcribed. Nothing else you say is analysed.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Settings.ThresholdExplanation">Below {0}: ignored without a word.  From {0} to {1}: Optimus offers and waits for “confirm”.  Above {1}: executed.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Settings.PiperMissing">Piper is not installed. Put piper.exe and at least one .onnx model in {0} — the binary comes from the rhasspy/piper releases, the voices from huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Settings.PiperOn">The model runs on this machine: nothing goes over the network. Reckon on about 400 ms per reply with a “medium” voice, against 10 ms for a Windows voice. Since speech comes after the action, that delay lands on the comment, never on the command. A “low” voice halves the wait.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Settings.WindowsVoice">Windows voices: near-instant (7 to 15 ms), always available, but synthetic in timbre. This is the safe choice.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Settings.CopilotMine">“{0}” is yours. It answers to “{1}”, and you can delete it — if it was shadowing a shipped copilot, the original comes back.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Settings.CopilotShipped">“{0}” ships with Optimus and cannot be deleted. It answers to “{1}”. Duplicate it to make one of your own: voice, character and replies follow, and you only have to bend them.</sys:String>
